  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Increasing forecast confidence that coastal \nlow pressure may bring rain to the southern Appalachians \nover the weekend.\n\nBenign weather continues through the work week with stout high \npressure overhear. This will support sunny days and mostly clear\nnights. This along with calm winds and low PWATs will result in\nanother couple of good radiational cooling nights and lows \nmainly in the low 20s to low 30s. A few colder outliers are \nexpected. We will also be a bit warmer on the ridgetops. This \npattern weakens by Thursday night, as the high's center shifts \ntowards the coast, allowing for southerly to easterly flow to \nbegin to bring low level moisture towards into the interior Mid-\nAtlantic. This will limit cooling some, so expect somewhat \nwarmer overnight lows. Cloud cover gradually ramps up Friday \ninto the weekend with a strengthening coastal low off GA and the\nCarolinas. \n\nBy then we will be have cold air damming occurring along the\nsouthern and central Appalachians. The coastal system will \ninteract with a surface baroclinic zone and mid level energy in \nthe area. Depending on where these features end up, we may see \ncold stratiform rain spread into the region, perhaps from late \nSaturday night through Sunday. QPF amounts look to be below a \nquarter inch at this point. Some light upslope showers and \nstratus look to persist into Monday if this scenario comes true.\nColder temperatures and dry weather return for the following \nwork week. &&",
